        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.668,
            0.065,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Perimeter and Area"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.12,
            0.302,
            0.142
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.151,
            0.755,
            0.172
          ],
          "content": "Cut out the tangram pieces given at the end of your textbook."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.179,
            0.58,
            0.336
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_10_chart_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.352,
            0.795,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": "1. Explore and figure out how many pieces have the same area."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.379,
            0.834,
            0.422
          ],
          "content": "2. How many times bigger is Shape D as compared to Shape C? What is the relationship between Shapes C, D and E?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.427,
            0.834,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "3. Which shape has more area: Shape D or F? Give reasons for your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.475,
            0.833,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "4. Which shape has more area: Shape F or G? Give reasons for your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.523,
            0.833,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": "5. What is the area of Shape A as compared to Shape G? Is it twice as big? Four times as big?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.574,
            0.835,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": "Hint: In the tangram pieces, by placing the shapes over each other, we can find out that Shapes A and B have the same area, Shapes C and E have the same area. You would have also figured out that Shape D can be exactly covered using Shapes C and E, which means Shape D has twice the area of Shape C or shape E, etc."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.709,
            0.833,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": "6. Can you now figure out the area of the big square formed with all seven pieces in terms of the area of Shape C?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.756,
            0.834,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": "7. Arrange these 7 pieces to form a rectangle. What will be the area of this rectangle in terms of the area of Shape C now? Give reasons for your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.827,
            0.835,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": "8. Are the perimeters of the square and the rectangle formed from these 7 pieces different or the same? Give an explanation for your answer."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.886,
            0.942,
            0.917,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": ""
